---
/**Story: Where Your Tax Dollar Goes.*/
import Story from '../../layouts/Story.astro';
import TaxDollarChart from '../../components/TaxDollarChart.tsx';
import SimpleBars from '../../components/SimpleBars.tsx';
import { BUDGET_2026 } from '../../lib/budget-year';
import { operating, type DeptBudgetRow } from '../../lib/department-budgets';

const budgets = BUDGET_2026.views.departmentBudgets as DeptBudgetRow[];
const revenue = BUDGET_2026.views.revenueWaterfall as { label: string; amount: number }[];

// 2026 operating-department budgets sorted by amount (trust funds & appendices excluded)
const depts = operating(budgets)
  .filter(r => r.year === '2026 Budget')
  .sort((a, b) => b.amount - a.amount);
const total = depts.reduce((s, d) => s + d.amount, 0);

// Compute cents per dollar
const top10 = depts.slice(0, 10);
const otherTotal = depts.slice(10).reduce((s, d) => s + d.amount, 0);
const taxDollarData = [
  ...top10.map(d => ({ label: d.department, cents: (d.amount / total) * 100 })),
  ...(otherTotal > 0 ? [{ label: `All other (${depts.length - 10} depts)`, cents: (otherTotal / total) * 100 }] : []),
];

// Public safety rollup
const psSlugs = ['bureau-of-police', 'bureau-of-fire', 'bureau-of-emergency-medical-services'];
const psDepts = depts.filter(d => psSlugs.includes(d.slug));
const psTotal = psDepts.reduce((s, d) => s + d.amount, 0);
const psCents = ((psTotal / total) * 100).toFixed(0);

// Revenue total
const revenueTotal = revenue.reduce((s, r) => s + r.amount, 0);
const taxRevenue = revenue.find(r => r.label.includes('Tax'))?.amount || 0;
const taxPct = ((taxRevenue / revenueTotal) * 100).toFixed(0);

function fmtM(n: number): string {
  return `$${(n / 1_000_000).toFixed(1)}M`;
}

const perResident = Math.round(total / 302_971);
---

<Story
  budgetYear={BUDGET_2026}
  title="Where Your Tax Dollar Goes"
  subtitle="A cent-by-cent breakdown of Pittsburgh's operating budget"
  description="Every dollar the city collects, broken down by department. How much goes to police? Parks? What about debt?"
  date="March 2026"
>
  <p>
    If you're a Pittsburgh resident, you contribute to a <strong>{fmtM(total)}</strong> operating budget
    through taxes, fees, and other revenue. That's about <strong>${perResident.toLocaleString()} per person</strong>.
    But where does that money actually go?
  </p>

  <h2>For every dollar the city spends...</h2>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <TaxDollarChart data={taxDollarData} client:load />
  </div>

  <p>
    The chart above breaks down each dollar of city spending by department. The single largest
    recipient is the <strong>Department of Finance</strong>, which handles citywide expenses like
    debt service, pension contributions, and insurance — costs that aren't tied to any one department.
  </p>

  <h2>Public Safety: {psCents}¢ of every dollar</h2>

  <p>
    Combine Police ({((psDepts.find(d => d.slug === 'bureau-of-police')?.amount || 0) / total * 100).toFixed(0)}¢),
    Fire ({((psDepts.find(d => d.slug === 'bureau-of-fire')?.amount || 0) / total * 100).toFixed(0)}¢), and
    EMS ({((psDepts.find(d => d.slug === 'bureau-of-emergency-medical-services')?.amount || 0) / total * 100).toFixed(0)}¢)
    and <strong>public safety accounts for {psCents} cents of every dollar</strong> — the largest functional
    category by far. The Bureau of Police alone has 844 budgeted positions, making it the city's biggest employer.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Fun fact:</strong> For every dollar Pittsburgh spends on police, it spends about
      {((depts.find(d => d.slug === 'department-of-parks-and-recreation')?.amount || 0) /
        (psDepts.find(d => d.slug === 'bureau-of-police')?.amount || 1) * 100).toFixed(0)} cents on Parks and Recreation.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Where the revenue comes from</h2>

  <p>
    The money to fund all this comes primarily from <strong>taxes — {taxPct}% of total revenue</strong>.
    Earned income tax, property tax, and business taxes are the three pillars. The rest comes from
    state and federal intergovernmental transfers ({fmtM(revenue.find(r => r.label.includes('Intergovernmental'))?.amount || 0)}),
    fees for services ({fmtM(revenue.find(r => r.label.includes('Charges'))?.amount || 0)}),
    and smaller sources like permits, fines, and interest.
  </p>

  <p>
    For the full revenue breakdown and department-by-department detail, visit the
    <a href="/2026/operating/" class="text-blue-600 underline">2026 operating budget</a> or browse
    <a href="/2026/departments/" class="text-blue-600 underline">individual department summaries</a>.
  </p>
</Story>
